100 examples: 80 training, 20 holdout.

  leaked between train and holdout         0   ok
  duplicated inside training               0   ok
  wrong message shape                      0   ok
  most common family, share             0.59   LOOK

  the same checks after a careless split that reuses three holdout cases:

  leaked between train and holdout         3   LOOK
  duplicated inside training               0   ok
  wrong message shape                      0   ok
  most common family, share             0.59   LOOK

  written to data/meridian/finetune/train.jsonl and data/meridian/finetune/holdout.jsonl

Four checks, and the first one is the one that ruins projects.

A case in both sets makes your evaluation report memorisation as skill, and
the report will look excellent. It is the same failure as testing on your
training data in any other field, and it is easier to commit here because the
'data' is prose and the duplicate is a rephrasing rather than a copy — which
is why the check hashes a normalised question rather than the whole example.

On the size question: the honest answer is that hundreds of examples change
behaviour and thousands are needed to change it reliably, and that the number
matters far less than whether the examples agree with each other. Fifty
consistent examples of a house style beat five hundred that were written by
five people with different opinions — and an inconsistent training set is the
one failure mode that more data makes worse.

What this listing does not do is run the fine-tune. That is a training job
with a bill and a wait, and its result would be a model id that means nothing
to a reader. §30.4 is the part that matters anyway: whatever comes back gets
measured against the base model on Chapter 21's golden set, on cases the
training never saw, and the comparison is the deliverable rather than the
model.
